## 6. Create a Safe Test Directory

We will use a dedicated lab directory instead of changing system files.

```bash
mkdir -p ~/fim-lab
```

Create a test file:

```bash
echo "Original lab configuration" > ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

View its contents:

```bash
cat ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

Check its metadata:

```bash
ls -l ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

Calculate its SHA-256 hash:

```bash
sha256sum ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

Save the output. You will compare it with the hash after modifying the file.

---

## 7. Understand File Hashes

A cryptographic hash produces a value derived from file contents.

Example:

```text
File Contents:
Original lab configuration

        ↓ SHA-256

Hash:
[Your actual hash value]
```

Now modify the file:

```bash
echo "Updated lab configuration" > ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

Calculate the hash again:

```bash
sha256sum ~/fim-lab/test-config.txt
```

The hash should differ because the file contents changed.

A changed hash proves that the contents differ; it does not, by itself, prove that the modification was malicious.

